##Battleships
    The program simulates the game of Battleships, along with some statistics and Hill Cipher encryption.
    For its implementation, I used several functions:
    First, there are the functions allocate and free_mat, which dynamically allocate and free memory for a matrix, respectively. Then there are the functions ship_number,
ship_length and ship_name, which return the maximum number of a ship type, the length, and the name of a ship. The can_place function checks whether a ship can be placed
on the game board based on its coordinates and orientation, and afterward, the place_ship function places a ship on the board and saves its information —
type, starting coordinates, orientation, length, and whether it still has remaining cells. After that comes the print_bord function, which prints
each player's game board. The attack_process function is used to process each attack given by two coordinates. It checks whether the cell
has already been attacked, and if so it means the shot is a miss, otherwise the cell is marked as attacked. It checks whether a ship exists at that cell, and
if not, it means the shot was a miss, and if the ship exists but is already destroyed, again it means the shot was a miss; and if the ship exists, then it
checks the following: if the given coordinates represent the ship's head, then the shot destroys it, otherwise, if the coordinates are part of the ship,
it means the ship was hit, and if the ship's remaining cells are attacked, they are decremented until they reach 0, at which point it means the ship
is destroyed. Throughout the function, both the successful hits and the total shots are counted.
    Next come the functions used for statistics and for processing commands:
->"match_accuracy" is used to compute the accuracy of a single match
->"total_accuracy" is used to compute the total weighted accuracy across all matches
->"min_100" determines the minimum number of matches that would need to have 100% total accuracy so that the accuracy target is reached. For each match
the impact is computed, then based on this impact, the matches are sorted in descending order. After sorting, matches are set to 100% until the
target is reached, and the number of modified matches is returned.
->"min_hits" determines the minimum number of successful hits needed so that the target accuracy is reached. It checks how many hits are needed for
the desired accuracy and increments until it is reached. In the end, it returns the difference between the number of hits needed and the ones actually landed
->"max_0" determines the maximum number of matches that could have 0% accuracy such that the total accuracy doesn't drop below the given target. For
each match the impact it has is computed, and based on this impact, the matches are sorted in ascending order. After sorting, matches are set to 0% until
the accuracy drops below the desired target, meaning the needed number has been obtained.
->"max_hits" determines how many hits could be missed while still keeping the desired accuracy target. It checks the minimum number of hits needed so
that the desired accuracy remains, checking when the target is reached. Each time it isn't reached, the number of hits is incremented,
and in the end the difference between the hits landed in the match and the number obtained by the function is returned.
->"process_command" processes a received command and computes the statistics corresponding to it. The command read is "broken down" so we know which player
it's about, the type of accuracy (total or for a single match), the match index, the accuracy target, and whether it's a requirement involving
a maximum or a minimum. The 4 possible cases are handled (total accuracy and the minimum number of matches with 100% accuracy, accuracy for one match and
the minimum number of hits that can't be missed, total accuracy and the maximum number of matches that could have had 0% accuracy, and accuracy for a single
match and the maximum number of hits that could have been missed), and then the result and the accuracy are passed back through the output parameters.
    Next come the functions used for the final requirements, encryption and decryption:
"to_number" converts a character to a number according to the given alphabet, "to_char" converts a number to a character, "mod_37" applies the % 37 operation, both for
positive and negative numbers, "text_transformation" turns a string of characters into a vector of numbers, "build_key" builds a "key" matrix
(it goes through the key read via the "key" variable and builds an n x n matrix, where n is the square root of the key's length, whose rows and columns
hold the numbers representing each letter), "mat_mod37" multiplies a matrix by a column vector and applies mod 37 to each element, "multiply" multiplies
each element of a matrix by a scalar and applies mod 37 to it, "multiplicative" finds the multiplicative inverse of a number a mod 37, i.e. it looks for an i in the range
[1, 36] such that a multiplied by i mod 37 equals 1, "det_2" computes the determinant of a 2x2 matrix, "adj_2" computes the adjugate of a 2x2 matrix,
"inv_2" computes the inverse of a 2x2 matrix (finds the multiplicative inverse of the determinant, the adjugate, and then multiplies them), "det_3" computes the determinant of a
3x3 matrix, "adj_3" computes the adjugate of a 3x3 matrix, "inv_3" computes the inverse of a 3x3 matrix (finds the multiplicative inverse of the determinant, the adjugate,
and then multiplies them).
    The "encrypt" function receives a message that it will encrypt. It determines n, the square root of the key's length, and splits the message to be encrypted into several
chunks of length n. For each chunk, it turns the characters into a numeric vector, builds the key matrix, multiplies it by that vector,
applies mod 37, and converts the result back into characters.
    The "decrypt" function decrypts a received message, i.e. it essentially simulates the reverse process of encryption. It determines n, the square root of the key's length, and splits the received message
into several chunks of length n. For each chunk it turns the characters into a numeric vector, builds the key matrix and computes its inverse, depending
on its size, then multiplies the inverse by the numeric vector, and the result is afterward converted back into characters.
    The main program is in main. It starts by reading the number of games and dynamically allocating the vectors used for statistics. For each match
the board dimensions are read, the number of ships of each type is computed, and matrices are allocated for each player's board, as well as vectors to store
the specifications of each ship. Then, the ship-placement process alternates between players and continues until both have placed all their ships. Right after, the
boards are printed for each player. The next part of the program is used for processing attacks. These attacks also alternate between players
and stop once a player has no ships left, at which point it is clear the other player has won. The last part is dedicated to statistics and
encryption/decryption. The key is read, and depending on it there are two cases:
-first case, if the key is "-", it means the commands are not encrypted
-second case, the key is encrypted
    In both cases, commands are read until Q is encountered. Each command is processed by the "process_command" function and then
the results are printed, either plain or encrypted.
    In the case of encrypted commands, they are decrypted by calling the "decrypt" function, and at the end they are encrypted by calling the "encrypt"
function.
    For compiling and cleaning I used build and clean.
